WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.917
Operators need enough visibility to distinguish service problems, model problems,

00:00:03.917 --> 00:00:05.826
client problems, and hardware problems.

00:00:05.826 --> 00:00:10.406
When a local AI workflow fails, the visible symptom may be vague:

00:00:10.406 --> 00:00:13.841
a slow response, a missing model, an application timeout,

00:00:13.841 --> 00:00:17.277
or a user report that the tool feels different.

00:00:17.277 --> 00:00:21.857
Troubleshooting is much easier when the environment has a simple operating picture.

00:00:21.857 --> 00:00:26.056
Useful troubleshooting starts with service status, logs, model inventory, storage availability,

00:00:26.056 --> 00:00:27.201
and resource utilization.

00:00:27.201 --> 00:00:28.728
Is the service running?

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:30.636
Is the expected model present?

00:00:30.636 --> 00:00:32.545
Is there enough disk space?

00:00:32.545 --> 00:00:34.072
Is memory under pressure?

00:00:34.072 --> 00:00:36.744
Is CPU or GPU utilization unusually high?

00:00:36.744 --> 00:00:38.652
Did a configuration value change?

00:00:38.652 --> 00:00:44.759
These basic checks can explain many issues before teams start changing application code or model settings.

00:00:44.759 --> 00:00:48.958
Slow responses may come from model loading, insufficient memory, long prompts,

00:00:48.958 --> 00:00:53.157
high concurrency, CPU or GPU pressure, thermal throttling, or client-side expectations.

00:00:53.157 --> 00:00:57.737
Failed pulls or missing models may point to connectivity, permissions, naming, storage,

00:00:57.737 --> 00:00:59.264
or environment configuration issues.

00:00:59.264 --> 00:01:04.608
A troubleshooting checklist can reduce repeated support work and help new operators build confidence.

00:01:04.608 --> 00:01:08.043
Logging should support investigation without creating a data leak.

00:01:08.043 --> 00:01:11.860
Logs may reveal service errors, model loading behavior, configuration problems,

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:13.005
or integration failures.

00:01:13.005 --> 00:01:17.204
They should not casually store secrets, credentials, personal data, confidential prompts,

00:01:17.204 --> 00:01:18.349
or sensitive outputs.

00:01:18.349 --> 00:01:22.930
The best troubleshooting practice is to gather enough evidence to act while

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:25.220
still respecting privacy and data boundaries.
